{"authored" [{"question" "q-40264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖u‖=15$ and $‖u+\\sqrt{5}v‖=75.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖v‖.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040753/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c8d5431f2416a11a2ffd140598219391744997246558f294f0abd839312a40a" "basis" 1467 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040753/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-40264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $u$ is orthogonal to $\\sqrt{5}v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+\\sqrt{5}v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+‖\\sqrt{5}v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+\\sqrt{5}v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5}‖v‖)^{2} \\\\ ‖u+\\sqrt{5}v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+5‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (75)^{2} & =(15)^{2}+5‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 5\\,625 & =225+5‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 5\\,400 & =5‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ ‖v{‖}^{2} & =1\\,080\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $‖v‖=6\\sqrt{30}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040753/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c8d5431f2416a11a2ffd140598219391744997246558f294f0abd839312a40a" "basis" 1467 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040753/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-40264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040753/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6297f9e56cb57b1d79f5f50f4f410d895cc53b635661ae753c51f260a8a7e2bc"