lution" "value" "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖4u+v‖ & \\le ‖4u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖4u+v‖ & \\le 4‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 22 & \\le 4‖u‖+2 \\\\ 20 & \\le 4‖u‖ \\\\ 5 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e2f7c011c28a30d158917074d36797a8cff9e5991a121e9ce0a158de43205903"} {"question" "q-40197" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40197" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40197" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $‖9u+v‖=23$ and $‖v‖=5$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖9u+v‖ & \\le ‖9u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖9u+v‖ & \\le 9‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 23 & \\le 9‖u‖+5 \\\\ 18 & \\le 9‖u‖ \\\\ 2 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e2f7c011c28a30d158917074d36797a8cff9e5991a121e9ce0a158de43205903"} {"question" "q-40901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $|u\\cdot v|+‖u+v‖=19$ and $‖v‖=9$, then what is the smallest possible value of $‖u‖?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19 & =|u\\cdot v|+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & =‖u‖\\cdot (9)+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & =9‖u‖+‖u+v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the triangle inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9‖u‖+‖u+v‖ & \\le 9‖u‖+(‖u‖+‖v‖) \\\\  & =9‖u‖+(‖u‖+9) \\\\  & =10‖u‖+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two inequalities, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19 & \\le 10‖u‖+9 \\\\ 10 & \\le 10‖u‖ \\\\ 1 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40243" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e2f7c011c28a30d158917074d36797a8cff9e5991a121e9ce0a158de43205903"} {"question" "q-40243" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40243" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40243" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-39608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $|u\\cdot v|+‖u+v‖=34$ and $‖v‖=6$, then what is the smallest possible value of $‖u‖?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-39608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}34 & =|u\\cdot v|+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & =‖u‖\\cdot (6)+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & =6‖u‖+‖u+v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the triangle inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6‖u‖+‖u+v‖ & \\le 6‖u‖+(‖u‖+‖v‖) \\\\  & =6‖u‖+(‖u‖+6) \\\\  & =7‖u‖+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two inequalities, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}34 & \\le 7‖u‖+6 \\\\ 28 & \\le 7‖u‖ \\\\ 4 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-39608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e2f7c011c28a30d158917074d36797a8cff9e5991a121e9ce0a158de43205903"} {"question" "q-39608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-39608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "14"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-39608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-74193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let the vectors $v$ and $w$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2}=158$ and $‖u‖=‖w‖=5$, then what is the minimum possible value of $‖v‖?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-74193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}158 & ={|u\\cdot v|}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={5}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =25‖v{‖}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v⊥w$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25‖v{‖}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =25‖v{‖}^{2}+2(‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2}) \\\\  & =25‖v{‖}^{2}+2(‖v{‖}^{2}+{5}^{2}) \\\\  & =27‖v{‖}^{2}+50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}158 & \\le 27‖v‖+50 \\\\ 108 & \\le 27‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 4 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $4\\le ‖v{‖}^{2}$ implies that $2\\le ‖v‖.$ Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $‖v‖$ is $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-74193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e2f7c011c28a30d158917074d36797a8cff9e5991a121e9ce0a158de43205903"} {"question" "q-74193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-74193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-74193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $|u\\cdot v|+‖u+v‖=12\\sqrt{3}$ and $‖v‖=2\\sqrt{3}$, then what is the smallest possible value of $‖u‖?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\sqrt{3} & =|u\\cdot v|+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & =‖u‖\\cdot (2\\sqrt{3})+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}‖u‖+‖u+v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the triangle inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}‖u‖+‖u+v‖ & \\le 2\\sqrt{3}‖u‖+(‖u‖+‖v‖) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}‖u‖+(‖u‖+2\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{3}+1)‖u‖+2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two inequalities, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\sqrt{3} & \\le (2\\sqrt{3}+1)‖u‖+2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 10\\sqrt{3} & \\le (2\\sqrt{3}+1)‖u‖ \\\\ \\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}+1} & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}+1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e2f7c011c28a30d158917074d36797a8cff9e5991a121e9ce0a158de43205903"} {"question" "q-40242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}+1}"] ["math" "\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-40242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}+1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-140462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let the vectors $v$ and $w$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2}=204$ and $‖u‖=4$ and $‖w‖=7$, then what is the minimum possible value of $‖v‖?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-140462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}204 & =|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v⊥w$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16‖v{‖}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+3(‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2}) \\\\  & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+3(‖v{‖}^{2}+{7}^{2}) \\\\  & =19‖v{‖}^{2}+147.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}204 & \\le 19‖v‖+147 \\\\ 57 & \\le 19‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 3 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $3\\le ‖v{‖}^{2}$ implies that $\\sqrt{3}\\le ‖v‖.$ Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $‖v‖$ is $\\sqrt{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-140462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e2f7c011c28a30d158917074d36797a8cff9e5991a121e9ce0a158de43205903"} {"question" "q-140462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-140462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "q-140462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/state.json" "file_sha256" "797dff20b4319f1b37405a7d50b7088a0d980fa80dd36e79e0dabf7a44fee50d"} {"question" "e-5557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ be vectors such that $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert = 6$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{u}+4\\mathbf{v} \\Vert = 25.$  If $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v},$ find $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/example-5557.json" "file_sha256" "04030ddc7c945ea4d999fb5c736a86be4137a711b98b65e43c14b2954e57c403"} {"question" "e-5557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v},$ we also have that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $4\\mathbf{v}.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+4v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+‖4v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+4v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+(4‖v‖)^{2} \\\\ ‖u+4v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+16‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (25)^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+16(6)^{2} \\\\ 625 & =‖u{‖}^{2}+576 \\\\ ‖u{‖}^{2} & =625-576 \\\\ ‖u{‖}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}=\\Vert\\mathbf{u}\\Vert^2=49.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/example-5557.json" "file_sha256" "04030ddc7c945ea4d999fb5c736a86be4137a711b98b65e43c14b2954e57c403"} {"question" "e-5558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} +3\\mathbf{v} \\Vert=14$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert=2,$ find the smallest possible value of $\\Vert \\mathbf{u}\\Vert.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/example-5558.json" "file_sha256" "25d9178f8c7d0d725c9e1eadef3d388961374ec4644108443f869195004d90c3"} {"question" "e-5558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+3v‖ & \\le ‖u‖+‖3v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+3v‖ & \\le ‖u‖+3‖v‖ \\\\ 14 & \\le ‖u‖+3(2) \\\\ 14 & \\le ‖u‖+6 \\\\ 8 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert$ is $8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/example-5558.json" "file_sha256" "25d9178f8c7d0d725c9e1eadef3d388961374ec4644108443f869195004d90c3"} {"question" "e-5559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let the vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}|^2+5\\Vert\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w}\\Vert^2=29$ and $\\| \\mathbf{u} \\|  = \\| \\mathbf{w} \\| = 2$, then what is the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/example-5559.json" "file_sha256" "5d4ca2ff4d4b5d5d0fd7c45d7fb0b8b631aee96b1db84bbfe5ce24ed7afb5a64"} {"question" "e-5559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}29 & =|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =4‖v{‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4‖v{‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =4‖v{‖}^{2}+5(‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2}) \\\\  & \\le 4‖v{‖}^{2}+5(‖v{‖}^{2}+{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =9‖v{‖}^{2}+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}29 & \\le 9‖v{‖}^{2}+20 \\\\ 9 & \\le 9‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 1 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert^2$ implies that $1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$. Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$ is $1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048257/example-5559.json" "file_sha256" "5d4ca2ff4d4b5d5d0fd7c45d7fb0b8b631aee96b1db84bbfe